  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: An upper-level area of high pressure \nwill remain over the Southeastern United States Sunday. Southerly\nflow should keep monsoonal moisture streaming northward into \nEastern Nevada today, resulting in isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms this afternoon into this evening. A few of these \nthunderstorms could be strong. The primary thunderstorm hazards \nwill be erratic wind gusts near 50 mph and dangerous cloud to \nground lightning strikes. There is a marginal risk (5-15%) of \nexcessive rainfall in Southern White Pine and Eastern Nye Counties\nthis afternoon into this evening. Heavy rainfall may result in \nflooding of recent burn scars. The setup is very similar on Monday\nwith slightly better CAPE values (250-300 J/kg) over central NV.\nThe moisture profile (0.60-0.80 inches) indicates a mix of more\nwet than dry storm activity. Brief heavy rains especially at \nhigher elevations are possible through Monday evening.\n\nThe aforementioned anticyclone aloft will progressive retrograde\nwestward Sunday through Friday. As a result, the flow should shift\nfrom southerly to southwesterly, pushing monsoonal moisture\neastward. There is an elevated risk of wildfires in Humboldt\nCounty Monday afternoon into Monday evening due to southwest wind\ngusts near 25 mph and minimum relative humidity values as low as\n12 percent. A warming trend is expected Sunday through Thursday.\nBy Thursday afternoon, high temperatures across Northern and\nCentral Nevada will be two to four degrees above normal for this\ntime of year. High temperatures will remain above seasonal values\nFriday. Here are probabilities of maximum temperatures of 90\ndegrees or higher for select locations in Northern and Central\nNevada on Thursday, August 20th, 2026: \n\nBattle Mountain - 96%\nElko - 95%\nWinnemucca - 95%\nWest Wendover - 95%\nCarlin - 95%\nTonopah - 90%\nWells - 90%\nSpring Creek - 85%\nEureka - 85%\nEly - 70%\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence\nregarding the possibility of a few strong thunderstorms in Eastern\nNevada Sunday afternoon and evening. Moderate forecast confidence\nregarding a warming trend Sunday through Thursday. Low forecast \nconfidence in elevated fire weather conditions in Humboldt County \nMonday. Pops were increased and isolated thunderstorms were added\nto Wx Grids through 12Z Sunday as satellite & radar imagery\ndepicted storms initiating and strengthening over Elko County. NBM\ngrids had no shower or thunderstorm activity in that region.",
